---
title: "Secrets"
description: "How to store a password, API key, or database credential once in your Mac's keychain, and how Piper hands it to the one command that needs it without seeing the value itself."
---

Store a database password once and every later task can query that database without you pasting the password again. Piper puts the value in your Mac's keychain and hands it straight to the one command that needs it. The value never comes back to you on screen, and Piper's own work never holds it.

## What a secret is

A **secret** is a named bundle of credentials you save in Piper. Each bundle holds one or more **fields**, and a field is a short key with a secret value behind it. A warehouse login might be one bundle named `Warehouse` with the fields `HOST`, `USERNAME`, and `PASSWORD`. A single API key is a bundle with one field.

In **Settings**, select **Secrets** to see the bundles you have saved. Each one shows its name, its description, and how many fields it holds. No value is ever shown.

## Where the value lives

The value goes into your Mac's keychain, one keychain item per field. Piper's own database keeps the bundle's name, its description, and the field keys, and holds no values at all. Nothing in Piper's interface can read a value back out.

<Warning>
  Piper never shows a secret's value again after you save it. To change a stored field, type a new value over the placeholder in its box. If you lose the original value, get a new one from the service and replace the field.
</Warning>

## Store a credential

<Steps>
  <Step title="Open Secrets">
    In **Settings**, select **Secrets**.
  </Step>
  <Step title="Start a new secret">
    Select **New secret**.
  </Step>
  <Step title="Name it">
    Give it a name you will recognize later, like `Warehouse`. Add a description if it helps, such as `Snowflake prod, read-only`.
  </Step>
  <Step title="Add the fields">
    Type a key, like `PASSWORD`, then its value. A key must start with a letter and use only letters, digits, and underscores, and Piper stores it in capitals. Select **Add field** for each further field.
  </Step>
  <Step title="Save">
    Select **Save secret**. Every value goes to the keychain at that moment.
  </Step>
</Steps>

## How Piper uses one

You do not do anything special. Ask for the work in plain words, and Piper looks up which secrets are stored, matches the one your task needs, and runs the command that needs the credential with the value handed to it directly.

That handover is narrow on purpose. The value reaches exactly one command, for as long as that command runs, and never enters the rest of Piper's work. Before that command's output comes back, Piper replaces every secret value in it with `•••`, so a credential does not travel on into a chart, a file Piper writes, or a reply.

<Note>
  Very short values are left alone in command output: anything under four characters, and plain numbers under six digits such as a port number. Masking those would blank out ordinary results.
</Note>

If your task needs a credential you have not stored yet, Piper names the missing one and stops. Add it in **Settings** > **Secrets**, then ask again.

## Change or remove one

Select the pencil icon on a bundle to edit it. You can change the name, the description, and the values, and you can add new fields. An existing field's key is fixed, and its value stays as it is unless you type a new one over it.

Select the trash icon to delete a bundle, then confirm. Piper removes the bundle and its stored values for good.

<Tip>
  Give each bundle a name that says what it opens, not what kind of thing it is. `Warehouse` and `Stripe live` tell you what you are looking at later. `Key 1` does not.
</Tip>

